From: Anders Sandberg <nv91-asa@nada.kth.se>
Date: Wed, 30 Nov 1994 10:53:50 +0100 (MET)
Subject: [#94-11-517] Green Goo

Nancy Lebovitz wrote:

>I've been having my doubts about "gray goo"--I suspect that a gray goo
>would be *very* hard to design--not the kind of thing a kid would come
>up with for a practical joke.

That is probably true. Gray goo requires a lot of flexibility to work in 
a natural environment. But Green Goo doesn't, it just has to be deadly or 
sterilizing in a specific type of organisms. We don't need nanotechnology 
for this, just some clever genetic engineering (or just finding a natural 
disease with the right properties).

>Also, as far as I can tell, nothing but governments really indulge in
>efforts to kill huge numbers of people. Terrorists only seem to want
>to kill smallish numbers for publicity.

Yes, most terrorists seem to like dramatic, very local attacks. But as 
technology develops, more and more people get access (and the necessary 
knowledge) of potentially dangerous technologies. This means that even a 
small group of people can unleash terrible destruction. Even if this is 
rare (there aren't that many evil geniuses around), there is a finite 
probability of such actions. 

In fact, this is my main argument for space colonization: survival.

