From: Webb_S <WebbS@oplc.psb.bls.gov>
Date: Wed, 30 Nov 94 09:27:00 EST
Subject: [#94-11-520] GENE: Resurrecting the dodo?

Craig Presson wrote:

>Cope's rule is "of thumb" like most such rules. Another violation is
>today's pachyderms, who evolved from much larger forms. When large size
>is maladaptive, large forms are selected out, either by extinction or
>natural selection.
>
>Also, there were many smaller species of dinosaur, the best-known of
>which is the chicken-sized Compsognathus.

Actually, pachyderms evolved from _smaller_ forms, as birds probably did. 
 Today's elephants evolved from Moetherium, a pig-sized animal which lived 
40 million years ago.  Twenty million years later, its descendant, 
Gompotherium, had grown to Indian elephant size.  The first true elephants 
Stegotetrabeldon (Indian-elephant size) and Primelephas (somewhat smaller) 
evolved around 6 m.y.a.  Certain mammoths, such as the giant M. 
trogontherii, grew to over 14' in height, compared to 10-11' for male 
African elephants and 9-10' for male Indian elephants.  However, mammoths 
evolved concurrently _with_ elephants, not as predecessors _to_ elephants.

Other pachyderms (rhinos and hippos) similarly had some very large cousins, 
some as much as 18' high at the shoulder.  However, as with elephants, these 
were not direct predecessors to today's forms but rather giant forms 
descended from a common (smaller) ancestor.

As for dinosaurs, forms as small as Compsognathus (4' long, 6 lbs.) were 
quite rare compared to the giant types.  Even as one of the tiniest 
dinosaurs, Compsognatus was still much larger than Archaeopteryx (1' long, 
0.5 lb.).  It wasn't particularly birdlke, either.  The smallest known 
protobird (dinosaur closely resembling a bird) was Troodon, which was 
significantly larger (6' long and 30-60 lbs).  Other protobirds such as 
Velociraptor were larger still.  The protobirds also evolved _after_ 
Archaeopteryx, even though they are much more birdlike than any dinosaur 
which came before.

The "dino-birds" Cosesaurus, Megalancosaurus, and Longisquima, on the other 
hand, were only 7-15" long, lightly built birdlike animals which lived 
_before_ Archaeopteryx.

It's true, as you say, that Cope's Rule is only a rule of thumb.  Under 
certain environmental conditions, such as a long period of isolation on an 
island, dwarf forms of an animal may develop.  Variations in temperature or 
diet may also affect size somewhat (note the variation in human size).  But 
there is no evidence that size was maladaptive in the case of 
dinosaurs/birds.  Therapods grew _larger_ throughout the Mesozoic, and 
Compsognathus and the protobirds lived on _after_ the evolution of 
Archaeopteryx.

Steven Webb
webbs@oplc.psb.bls.gov

